220
00:15:36,978 --> 00:15:42,019
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought I had it all set to go and ready, but I guess not.

221
00:15:44,790 --> 00:15:50,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, well- Chris, I'm sorry, could you just give a little background on this before we get started?

222
00:15:50,494 --> 00:15:51,255
[SPEAKER_08]: Is there- Yes, indeed.

223
00:15:51,535 --> 00:16:01,943
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay, so the Forest Street Historic District was proposed actually probably about three years ago.

224
00:16:03,884 --> 00:16:08,808
[SPEAKER_08]: And we've been moving very slowly on it, I think quite to the annoyance of the mayor.

225
00:16:11,021 --> 00:16:12,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Chris, I'm sorry for interrupting.

226
00:16:13,241 --> 00:16:37,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I ask why this wasn't done, you know, 50 years ago, or, you know, it seems like a new, you know, district, but, you know, these houses have been there for a long time, and I'm just trying to get maybe a little of the history of when or why this would have kind of come up as either a zoning thing to restrict, you know, future development or to really encapsulate historic, you know, properties.

227
00:16:38,281 --> 00:16:43,522
[SPEAKER_08]: Well, the Medford Historic District Commission has only existed since 1985.

228
00:16:43,582 --> 00:16:47,263
[SPEAKER_08]: So it couldn't have been done 50 years ago.

229
00:16:48,684 --> 00:16:51,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, was there any other way to sort of preserve that area?

230
00:16:51,424 --> 00:16:54,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Not really, no.

231
00:16:54,725 --> 00:17:05,348
[SPEAKER_08]: I mean, it could be zoned residential, which I'm not sure what exactly what the fine points of that are because there are some

232
00:17:06,648 --> 00:17:10,531
[SPEAKER_08]: residential houses that have been converted to commercial use.

233
00:17:12,172 --> 00:17:14,794
[SPEAKER_08]: There's the high school that has been converted to condos.

234
00:17:16,495 --> 00:17:24,560
[SPEAKER_08]: There's the Chevrolet theater, which is now city property, but no, no, I don't.

235
00:17:25,221 --> 00:17:28,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So why wouldn't like to say the Jonathan Wade house be in that?

236
00:17:28,463 --> 00:17:35,488
[SPEAKER_08]: I know it's a different street, but- That is part of the proposed Medford Square historic district.

237
00:17:37,802 --> 00:17:41,023
[SPEAKER_08]: And so that is another thing we will be dealing with this year.

238
00:17:43,264 --> 00:17:55,288
[SPEAKER_08]: We have two proposed historic districts, one for South Street, including Grandfather's House 21 Turo and Sharon Guzik's House 10 Manning.

239
00:17:58,129 --> 00:18:02,831
[SPEAKER_08]: And there's also a proposed, there has been for several years, a,

240
00:18:07,371 --> 00:18:17,219
[SPEAKER_08]: a proposed Medford Square historic district, which is part of the Medford Square master plan, which was proposed under Mayor McGlynn.

241
00:18:19,581 --> 00:18:25,866
[SPEAKER_08]: I don't know how much of the master plan as a whole has been implemented, but the historic district certainly has not.

242
00:18:28,348 --> 00:18:30,710
[SPEAKER_08]: And so the Jonathan White House would be part of that.

243
00:18:34,039 --> 00:18:37,421
[SPEAKER_08]: as would, as I hope will be the David Osgood House.

244
00:18:39,242 --> 00:18:40,262
[SPEAKER_08]: The Andrew House.

245
00:18:41,443 --> 00:18:43,744
[SPEAKER_08]: There are many historic buildings in Medford Square.

246
00:18:45,385 --> 00:18:46,486
[SPEAKER_08]: Some of them quite beautiful.

247
00:18:47,827 --> 00:18:53,770
[SPEAKER_08]: And for example, the building that the Bank of America is in, I don't recall its name, but

248
00:18:54,901 --> 00:19:03,804
[SPEAKER_08]: If you look above the, you know, the plate glass storefronts and look at the top of the building, it's just a gorgeous piece of Victorian architecture.

249
00:19:03,904 --> 00:19:04,824
[SPEAKER_01]: The old opera house.

250
00:19:05,664 --> 00:19:05,884
[SPEAKER_08]: Yeah.

251
00:19:06,204 --> 00:19:06,304
[SPEAKER_08]: Yeah.

252
00:19:06,324 --> 00:19:06,904
[SPEAKER_08]: I think that's it.

253
00:19:07,124 --> 00:19:09,105
[SPEAKER_08]: Yes.

254
00:19:10,625 --> 00:19:13,426
[SPEAKER_08]: So we want to do a historic district from Everett Square.

255
00:19:13,446 --> 00:19:14,607
[SPEAKER_08]: This is not what this is.

256
00:19:14,807 --> 00:19:15,227
[SPEAKER_08]: This is a,

257
00:19:17,026 --> 00:19:19,768
[SPEAKER_08]: historic district of Forest Street.

258
00:19:20,989 --> 00:19:23,911
[SPEAKER_08]: Do you know the whole background of Forest Street, Chris?

259
00:19:24,471 --> 00:19:24,772
[SPEAKER_08]: I do.

260
00:19:25,472 --> 00:19:25,752
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay.

261
00:19:26,012 --> 00:19:34,598
[SPEAKER_08]: I mean, I know bunches of it, but- Yeah, basically Forest Street was like the Sunset Boulevard of Medford in 1900.

262
00:19:34,698 --> 00:19:45,285
[SPEAKER_08]: And it was lined with beautiful mansions, all built around those times, mainly built around that time, but some going maybe early Victorian.

263
00:19:48,024 --> 00:19:50,645
[SPEAKER_08]: And most of those buildings are gone.

264
00:19:51,985 --> 00:19:53,786
[SPEAKER_08]: There are probably 10 of them left.

265
00:19:54,906 --> 00:19:58,168
[SPEAKER_08]: And then there are other houses that after they were turned down were built.

266
00:19:59,008 --> 00:20:03,750
[SPEAKER_08]: And those are houses mainly built after the sort of golden age of forestry.

267
00:20:05,131 --> 00:20:13,274
[SPEAKER_08]: So it's actually kind of an iffy proposition because a lot of the houses are new and a lot of the residents don't wanna be in historic district, as I mentioned earlier.

