00:24:45,078 --> 00:24:58,323
[SPEAKER_08]: I mean, one of the things I mentioned in my email, which I apologize for sending out on late just recently is, of the houses that are in Fred's new district, six people voted no.

314
00:25:00,523 --> 00:25:04,685
[SPEAKER_08]: And they're all in houses without form Bs, except 33.

315
00:25:06,665 --> 00:25:08,026
[SPEAKER_08]: So I think if we,

316
00:25:08,868 --> 00:25:21,824
[SPEAKER_01]: If I could take it, if I ask maybe again, a rudimentary kind of question, as I'm reading this sort of the statutes, it doesn't seem like people are really being tied down with a lot of restrictive actions.

317
00:25:22,265 --> 00:25:23,947
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay, well, yes, amen.

318
00:25:27,906 --> 00:25:55,840
[SPEAKER_01]: I was, I was really kind of shocked when there was a lot of, you know, first floor exceptions, a lot of, you know, you know, unless it's seen from the public, which Brad's, you know, I heard that term, you know, we can't really see it and it replicates something not to, you know, to allow a lot of that, but there's a lot of, you know, exceptions for that, which I thought didn't really hamstring a homeowner in a district that

319
00:25:57,049 --> 00:25:59,711
[SPEAKER_01]: may not think their house is really representative of that.

320
00:26:00,452 --> 00:26:08,998
[SPEAKER_01]: So I was originally thinking to limit it more because, you know, that's a lot of restrictions, but I didn't see a whole lot of restrictions and I may be wrong.

321
00:26:09,519 --> 00:26:10,339
[SPEAKER_08]: So- No, you're right.

322
00:26:10,420 --> 00:26:11,240
[SPEAKER_08]: You're absolutely right.

323
00:26:12,581 --> 00:26:20,848
[SPEAKER_01]: And- So what do we say to those, as an elevator talk to those families saying, you know, that it's too restrictive?

324
00:26:24,130 --> 00:26:26,312
[SPEAKER_08]: You know, this is something we're going to learn from our consultants.

325
00:26:26,880 --> 00:26:40,949
[SPEAKER_08]: And from you, we have welcome ideas from anybody's ideas on this, but I didn't think we had really massive opposition to the old boundaries.

326
00:26:41,369 --> 00:26:47,433
[SPEAKER_08]: And we're just trying to salvage something at this point, which I'd like to consider in more detail at our next meeting.

327
00:26:48,380 --> 00:26:54,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And I don't want to bring up anything wider, Fred, because I know there's probably a ton of work done on every one of those districts.

328
00:26:54,083 --> 00:27:02,508
[SPEAKER_01]: But as I've just found out through the years, there was more and more to the stories that I was always fascinated by.

329
00:27:02,528 --> 00:27:07,070
[SPEAKER_01]: There was always another building, another, you know, say the Jonathan Wade House.

330
00:27:07,090 --> 00:27:08,991
[SPEAKER_01]: It just never made sense to me.

331
00:27:09,712 --> 00:27:17,616
[SPEAKER_01]: So as I look at these districts and I understand the traffic lane, the signage maybe for what the district is, but

332
00:27:18,127 --> 00:27:34,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Like I say, looking around Ashland place and a couple of them as I dig in deeper into inventory, one of those districts a little bit more dynamic to create some sort of tone of what Medford Common was.

333
00:27:34,451 --> 00:27:36,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Medford Common, I didn't remember we heard that term.

334
00:27:36,912 --> 00:27:39,272
[SPEAKER_08]: So- Chris, we need to move on.

335
00:27:39,292 --> 00:27:39,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, sorry.

336
00:27:40,253 --> 00:27:40,933
[SPEAKER_08]: Yeah, quite all right.

337
00:27:42,316 --> 00:27:55,407
[SPEAKER_08]: Now, let's go back to our, so we will talk about this in more detail at our next meeting, and hopefully our consultant will have been weighed in on some strategic questions on how to propose this.

