00:46:53,840 --> 00:46:54,860
[SPEAKER_08]: Yes, of course, Marianne.

547
00:46:55,924 --> 00:46:57,245
[SPEAKER_09]: Yes, go ahead.

548
00:46:57,726 --> 00:47:00,568
[SPEAKER_09]: My name is Angela Murphy, and I also live on Conning Street.

549
00:47:01,609 --> 00:47:05,692
[SPEAKER_09]: And I want to chime in because I live in a historic house.

550
00:47:05,792 --> 00:47:11,117
[SPEAKER_09]: It's not known to be, but I know it's over 300 years old, and I'm trying to find out exactly.

551
00:47:11,597 --> 00:47:12,758
[SPEAKER_09]: And it's on Conning Street.

552
00:47:13,739 --> 00:47:20,045
[SPEAKER_09]: And I'm just saying any changes inside can be reversed.

553
00:47:20,465 --> 00:47:23,928
[SPEAKER_09]: But once you change the outside, and if the house is intact,

554
00:47:24,607 --> 00:47:30,792
[SPEAKER_09]: as it is or was when it was built, then that's undoable.

555
00:47:31,933 --> 00:47:33,975
[SPEAKER_09]: Again, you can't reverse that after.

556
00:47:35,336 --> 00:47:47,346
[SPEAKER_09]: It's taking away the integrity of how the house looked when it was originally built in the 1700s and owned by Abigail Adams.

557
00:47:47,386 --> 00:47:49,508
[SPEAKER_09]: That's what we're concerned about because

558
00:47:50,062 --> 00:47:56,084
[SPEAKER_09]: Method is proud of its history, and why I didn't know about this, lived in my house for 66 years.

559
00:47:56,624 --> 00:48:02,206
[SPEAKER_09]: And why I didn't know about this house, it's like Method forgot its history.

560
00:48:02,306 --> 00:48:03,246
[SPEAKER_09]: And this is important.

561
00:48:03,266 --> 00:48:11,729
[SPEAKER_09]: This is John Adams, John Quincy Adams, two presidents of the United States, owned that property, paid taxes on that property.

562
00:48:12,450 --> 00:48:19,392
[SPEAKER_09]: And I think it's a disrespect to our history and our knowledge that Method was part of their

563
00:48:20,608 --> 00:48:23,509
[SPEAKER_09]: Um, land and their, their legacy.

564
00:48:23,929 --> 00:48:28,431
[SPEAKER_09]: I mean, if this was Quincy, they'd be all over this, but it's not.

565
00:48:28,591 --> 00:48:37,635
[SPEAKER_09]: And Method seems to not care because they've already torn down two houses on Cotting Street that were, should have been historic.

566
00:48:38,435 --> 00:48:40,936
[SPEAKER_09]: We were all historic in this neighborhood.

567
00:48:41,816 --> 00:48:49,179
[SPEAKER_09]: And my house has original historic features in it still that we chose to preserve.

568
00:48:50,231 --> 00:48:52,972
[SPEAKER_09]: And I know we did remodel because we bought it in 1956.

569
00:48:54,732 --> 00:49:00,253
[SPEAKER_09]: But I wish I could put it back to what it was in 1956, but I can't.

570
00:49:01,014 --> 00:49:04,854
[SPEAKER_09]: And once you do the outside and change it, it's going to be like that.

571
00:49:05,615 --> 00:49:07,235
[SPEAKER_09]: And that's what we're concerned.

572
00:49:07,535 --> 00:49:09,236
[SPEAKER_09]: If you do something on the inside, fine.

573
00:49:09,656 --> 00:49:12,316
[SPEAKER_09]: You can always put it back and get the blueprints and do that.

574
00:49:12,356 --> 00:49:17,337
[SPEAKER_09]: But if you change the roof line and the outside, I think it's a disrespect for the history.

575
00:49:17,617 --> 00:49:19,778
[SPEAKER_09]: And I think Method is doing a disservice

576
00:49:20,152 --> 00:49:23,053
[SPEAKER_09]: to let this original house changed.

577
00:49:24,353 --> 00:49:25,293
[SPEAKER_09]: That's my comments.

578
00:49:26,053 --> 00:49:26,613
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay, thank you.

579
00:49:27,093 --> 00:49:31,754
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay, so I think we've heard from- I think David has more to share too.

580
00:49:31,934 --> 00:49:32,314
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, sure.

581
00:49:32,474 --> 00:49:33,034
[SPEAKER_08]: Okay, David.

582
00:49:34,535 --> 00:49:34,995
[SPEAKER_07]: Two points.

583
00:49:35,415 --> 00:49:38,195
[SPEAKER_07]: Number one, what we're proposing to do is add some dormers to this house.

584
00:49:39,475 --> 00:49:40,436
[SPEAKER_07]: We're not gonna tear it down.

585
00:49:40,856 --> 00:49:42,636
[SPEAKER_07]: We're not gonna wrap it in vinyl siding.

586
00:49:43,056 --> 00:49:44,816
[SPEAKER_07]: We're not gonna convert it to a CVS.

587
00:49:44,976 --> 00:49:46,357
[SPEAKER_07]: We're proposing adding some dormers.

588
00:49:47,517 --> 00:49:48,237
[SPEAKER_07]: We love this house.

589
00:49:49,421 --> 00:49:55,231
[SPEAKER_07]: Number two, gosh, whatever else has been said earlier on, this sure sounds like opposition to doing anything.

590
00:49:55,612 --> 00:49:57,655
[SPEAKER_07]: This does not sound like a low hurdle to clear.

591
00:49:57,975 --> 00:50:02,503
[SPEAKER_07]: This sounds like strident opposition to doing anything, which is a shame.

592
00:50:05,613 --> 00:50:08,697
[SPEAKER_01]: If I could interject for one second.

593
00:50:09,638 --> 00:50:19,027
[SPEAKER_01]: I love the passion of the two women who love their community and telling me something new about Abigail Adams and I am right with you and the reason why I'm here today.

594
00:50:19,648 --> 00:50:21,689
[SPEAKER_01]: is to try and bring some of this history back.

595
00:50:22,249 --> 00:50:27,751
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know it necessarily is going to impede putting a pair of dormers on a roof.

596
00:50:27,771 --> 00:50:36,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And as much as I study architecture, I'm still trying to figure out what makes a community, you know, piece of architecture sing for me.

597
00:50:36,433 --> 00:50:48,777
[SPEAKER_01]: But I want to let you know the people here that I want to try and bring some of that passion back to Medford to bring some of that historical imagery back, but it doesn't necessarily have to impede

598
00:50:49,257 --> 00:50:55,260
[SPEAKER_01]: a homeowner's chance to improve their house, but we do need to bring back that legacy.

599
00:50:55,320 --> 00:50:57,321
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think we can do both at the same time.

600
00:50:57,641 --> 00:51:01,042
[SPEAKER_01]: We'll see how that approaches, but there's some very intelligent people in this room.

601
00:51:01,062 --> 00:51:03,903
[SPEAKER_01]: It looks like an organized plan, passionate people.

602
00:51:04,544 --> 00:51:07,325
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think there's win, win, win in all of this.

603
00:51:11,208 --> 00:51:18,950
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay, so I think we've heard- I would like to just ask who on this call has looked at the plans?

604
00:51:19,070 --> 00:51:34,014
[SPEAKER_05]: Has everybody who's on the historic district commission actually looked at the latest round of the plans and have any of our neighbors, I hope our neighbors who are also living in very old homes, I would assume then they would also like to be in a historic district of one.

605
00:51:34,234 --> 00:51:37,494
[SPEAKER_05]: I can't understand why they wouldn't if they're so passionate about this.

606
00:51:40,213 --> 00:51:49,115
[SPEAKER_08]: I personally have not looked at the plans, and I'm very confident in Doug's, especially Doug Carr's ability to make a good judgment on this, and I trust him implicitly.

607
00:51:49,915 --> 00:51:54,476
[SPEAKER_08]: So this really has nothing to do with your plans in my mind.

608
00:51:54,496 --> 00:51:56,936
[SPEAKER_08]: This really has nothing to do with your current plans.

609
00:51:57,856 --> 00:51:59,076
[SPEAKER_10]: Well, that's what I was going to say.

610
00:51:59,157 --> 00:52:00,537
[SPEAKER_10]: I was going to say there's two issues here.

611
00:52:00,577 --> 00:52:04,898
[SPEAKER_10]: There's the renovation is one thing, but creating the historic district is another.

612
00:52:04,918 --> 00:52:05,698
[SPEAKER_10]: Yes.

613
00:52:05,718 --> 00:52:07,118
[SPEAKER_10]: So there's two separate things here.

614
00:52:08,966 --> 00:52:13,829
[SPEAKER_10]: So do you have to have both together, or can you have one without the other, or does it have to be both?

615
00:52:14,489 --> 00:52:16,970
[SPEAKER_08]: It doesn't have to be both at all.

616
00:52:19,712 --> 00:52:27,156
[SPEAKER_10]: So is the district commission open to the idea of creating a hysteric district here?

617
00:52:28,437 --> 00:52:29,898
[SPEAKER_08]: We have resident interest in that.

618
00:52:33,292 --> 00:52:35,874
[SPEAKER_08]: an appropriate house to put in a local house.

619
00:52:36,114 --> 00:52:42,519
[SPEAKER_08]: I mean, we don't have too many pre-Civil War houses in Medford, especially, and if we do have them, they're usually not in good shape.

620
00:52:43,620 --> 00:52:48,783
[SPEAKER_10]: Right, in fact, John Clemson said in his report that he thinks that this is the oldest surviving house on the hillside.

621
00:52:50,345 --> 00:52:53,067
[SPEAKER_08]: I don't know if that's true or not, but it could be.

622
00:52:53,087 --> 00:52:55,028
[SPEAKER_10]: Yeah, I think it's in the Farm B somewhere.

623
00:52:59,138 --> 00:53:09,305
[SPEAKER_08]: No, the question is not the existing, the proposed work, but rather whether we want to, you know, just keep a watchful eye on this property going forward.

624
00:53:10,305 --> 00:53:18,530
[SPEAKER_08]: And as I say, we have never, in my tenure, we have never rejected anyone's applications.

625
00:53:19,831 --> 00:53:22,013
[SPEAKER_08]: We just look for appropriate design and material.

626
00:53:22,033 --> 00:53:23,694
[SPEAKER_08]: So we're not at all against change.

627
00:53:25,515 --> 00:53:28,477
[SPEAKER_00]: But let me say- Can I just chime in because I think

628
00:53:29,553 --> 00:53:36,976
[SPEAKER_00]: there's a very specific set of pathways that we'd have to perform to get it recognized as a historic district.

629
00:53:37,476 --> 00:53:40,437
[SPEAKER_00]: And it ultimately has to be voted on by the city council.

630
00:53:40,457 --> 00:53:43,518
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically a political organization.

631
00:53:44,878 --> 00:53:57,603
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's a series of steps laid out by the Commonwealth of Massachusetts, not by the city of Medford, of public hearings and then votes and more

632
00:53:58,983 --> 00:54:04,604
[SPEAKER_00]: more paperwork and then ultimately has to be going in front of the city council and the city council has to vote.

633
00:54:05,325 --> 00:54:07,585
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not just a majority.

634
00:54:07,645 --> 00:54:08,765
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's a, what are they?

635
00:54:08,985 --> 00:54:09,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Two thirds.

636
00:54:10,366 --> 00:54:11,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's a high.

637
00:54:12,826 --> 00:54:13,907
[SPEAKER_10]: I'm reading from a document.

638
00:54:13,927 --> 00:54:14,547
[SPEAKER_10]: This is Mary Ann.

639
00:54:14,847 --> 00:54:22,029
[SPEAKER_10]: I'm reading from a document that was actually prepared when they were evaluating 21 Toro Ave back in 2014.

640
00:54:22,329 --> 00:54:27,510
[SPEAKER_10]: That's exactly when the Farm B report was done for 78 Cotting Street.

641
00:54:28,627 --> 00:54:39,951
[SPEAKER_10]: And at that time, I guess there was a lot of discussion about inventorying the historic properties around the city.

642
00:54:40,451 --> 00:54:42,472
[SPEAKER_10]: And I'm gonna read from a paragraph here.

643
00:54:42,492 --> 00:54:57,317
[SPEAKER_10]: It says, the Revitalized District Commission began planning to expand the protection offered to the city's significant sites in two existing LHD, that's historic districts, by adding a series of single building districts across the city.

644
00:54:57,742 --> 00:55:00,823
[SPEAKER_10]: initially focusing on the National Register listed properties.

645
00:55:01,284 --> 00:55:04,085
[SPEAKER_10]: And the City Council has encouraged them in this endeavor.

646
00:55:04,625 --> 00:55:21,572
[SPEAKER_10]: On August 12, 2014, the City Council passed a motion requesting that the Historic District Commission forward to the City Council a list of properties that should be eligible for single house historic districts and begin the process of establishing such districts.

647
00:55:21,892 --> 00:55:23,273
[SPEAKER_10]: Now, I don't know if that ever happened.

648
00:55:24,311 --> 00:55:30,533
[SPEAKER_10]: So the 78 Carding Street was inventoried at the same time, August, 2014.

649
00:55:31,153 --> 00:55:39,795
[SPEAKER_10]: So I would assume that that would be one of the properties that would have been forwarded to the city council to be evaluated for a single house historic district.

650
00:55:40,415 --> 00:55:48,437
[SPEAKER_10]: Now, obviously it didn't happen and I don't know why, but the city council has in the past has already agreed to do these things.

651
00:55:52,218 --> 00:55:52,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Only in theory.

652
00:55:54,551 --> 00:55:55,131
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're right.

653
00:55:55,532 --> 00:56:03,277
[SPEAKER_00]: But the truth is the city council, those properties would only be approved with the support of the homeowner.

654
00:56:03,558 --> 00:56:07,600
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think that we've the city councils of members have come to this commission.

655
00:56:08,201 --> 00:56:12,984
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's one of the bars that they whether they will fulfill that during their vote.

656
00:56:13,705 --> 00:56:20,670
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's one of the criteria they use and why we've had such struggles establishing districts because

657
00:56:22,121 --> 00:56:31,149
[SPEAKER_00]: The people who oppose these things tend to be very vocal, very passionate, and that doesn't really get you the two-thirds vote with the committee.

658
00:56:31,169 --> 00:56:37,875
[SPEAKER_00]: If they hear any sort of whiff of controversy, they tend to stay away from it for obvious reasons, because they're elected officials.

659
00:56:38,656 --> 00:56:40,677
[SPEAKER_00]: So these things tend to be very slow.

660
00:56:40,697 --> 00:56:45,822
[SPEAKER_00]: We have to follow a very specific set of guidelines, as I mentioned, set up by the state.

661
00:56:47,192 --> 00:56:48,914
[SPEAKER_00]: which takes time and effort on our part.

662
00:56:49,014 --> 00:56:52,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think you've done the right thing at this house.

663
00:56:53,397 --> 00:56:59,241
[SPEAKER_00]: I love to see it a historic district, but that's gonna be months to now to do all this, all the steps.

664
00:56:59,281 --> 00:57:12,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So if the historical commission has weighed in on their design criteria and you're making effort to meet those criteria, I think that's great.

665
00:57:13,515 --> 00:57:23,499
[SPEAKER_00]: if we can include it in a future historic district because it's in a neighborhood of South Street or wherever we're trying to establish, that's the time we should be pursuing these things.

666
00:57:23,539 --> 00:57:29,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So I appreciate all the passion and all the love for this home on both sides.

667
00:57:31,222 --> 00:57:38,786
[SPEAKER_00]: So we try to do our best, but we just have a handful of houses under protection in our districts right now.

668
00:57:38,846 --> 00:57:39,866
[SPEAKER_00]: We're trying to expand that

669
00:57:40,658 --> 00:57:44,119
[SPEAKER_00]: but it takes time and it takes a lot of effort to make that stuff happen.

670
00:57:45,700 --> 00:57:50,781
[SPEAKER_00]: So Fred- And then Miguel and Angela, please consider joining our commission.

671
00:57:50,821 --> 00:57:51,902
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd love to have your passion.

672
00:57:52,082 --> 00:57:52,582
[SPEAKER_08]: Yes, indeed.

673
00:57:52,662 --> 00:57:58,664
[SPEAKER_00]: And same for Dan, you guys are welcome to be a member of this commission and help us move this forward.

674
00:57:58,884 --> 00:57:59,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely.

675
00:58:00,704 --> 00:58:07,786
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, in case we haven't said it clearly enough, we really do want all of you to look at the plans and give us whatever feedback you have.

676
00:58:08,087 --> 00:58:09,347
[SPEAKER_03]: We would love to hear your feedback.

677
00:58:10,039 --> 00:58:22,624
[SPEAKER_05]: And I would also like to make the point that, again, we are wanting to make changes that actually looks nice around the house and things like that.

678
00:58:23,344 --> 00:58:25,405
[SPEAKER_05]: In some ways, going above and beyond.

679
00:58:26,525 --> 00:58:29,286
[SPEAKER_05]: And I'm sorry that our neighbors feel like this is a disrespect to Medford.

680
00:58:29,527 --> 00:58:31,827
[SPEAKER_05]: Again, we're involved with the history.

681
00:58:31,867 --> 00:58:32,528
[SPEAKER_05]: We respect it.

682
00:58:34,629 --> 00:58:37,190
[SPEAKER_08]: OK, so I think the thing to do here

683
00:58:40,486 --> 00:58:45,049
[SPEAKER_08]: is to leave this in the hands of the historical commission for the time being.

684
00:58:47,771 --> 00:58:50,353
[SPEAKER_10]: And- Well, how does it stack?

685
00:58:50,393 --> 00:58:52,174
[SPEAKER_10]: Does it stack with them or does it stack with you?

686
00:58:53,595 --> 00:59:00,300
[SPEAKER_08]: Well, I wanna consult with the historical commission as far as what they want us to do.

687
00:59:02,482 --> 00:59:07,105
[SPEAKER_08]: And I think consulting with the mayor would probably be a good idea as well.

688
00:59:08,432 --> 00:59:15,014
[SPEAKER_08]: Um, but, um, uh, you know, we are, we are here to do the will of the people.

689
00:59:15,715 --> 00:59:24,478
[SPEAKER_08]: Um, so, um, and obviously owner of opposition is a huge obstacle to, to creating any kind of historic districts.

690
00:59:24,518 --> 00:59:28,079
[SPEAKER_08]: We want people to be happy and we want, you know, uh, right.

691
00:59:28,119 --> 00:59:36,362
[SPEAKER_10]: But I mean, as long as the owners know that it doesn't prevent them from doing their renovation, you know, that's that, I think that was their primary concern.

692
00:59:37,932 --> 00:59:39,914
[SPEAKER_08]: Oh yeah, no, no, no.

693
00:59:39,954 --> 00:59:42,956
[SPEAKER_08]: We have no problem with appropriate renovation of any kind.

694
00:59:44,217 --> 00:59:48,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And we would do a very similar process to what the historical commission is doing.

695
00:59:48,260 --> 00:59:57,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Meet with you, review the plans, talk with your architect, raise concerns, get it out there in front of the public and try to modify.

696
00:59:57,807 --> 01:00:04,351
[SPEAKER_00]: What we do is try to have things, respect the historical integrity of both the neighborhood and the home.

697
01:00:05,272 --> 01:00:07,454
[SPEAKER_00]: But we know that these are modern times

698
01:00:08,552 --> 01:00:12,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Houses get old and they need renovations on a regular basis, we know that.

699
01:00:12,915 --> 01:00:21,122
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's part of our job is to be a design review board, but our jurisdiction is only in properties that are in historic districts.

700
01:00:21,962 --> 01:00:27,226
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when we have basically authority to have these hearings and do that.

701
01:00:27,667 --> 01:00:37,935
[SPEAKER_00]: So if it's not in a historic district, it's in the historical commission because it's buildings, properties older than 50 years, they have a demo delay ordinance that they can employ

702
01:00:38,993 --> 01:00:41,034
[SPEAKER_00]: pause things and to take a step back.

703
01:00:41,074 --> 01:00:42,094
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what they're doing here.

704
01:00:42,114 --> 01:00:54,260
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think that the city is already implementing the tools it has at its disposal to, you know, to help get this project done in a way that's helpful to your owners and to the city itself.

705
01:00:55,040 --> 01:01:00,483
[SPEAKER_10]: And hopefully- Does the commission know whether the 78 Carting Street was ever looked at before?

706
01:01:02,163 --> 01:01:06,045
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's gonna form D that we would have, and the historical commission have,

707
01:01:06,880 --> 01:01:10,382
[SPEAKER_00]: have tried to survey as many properties in the city as possible.

708
01:01:11,883 --> 01:01:16,886
[SPEAKER_10]: Yeah, but has this property ever been looked at to be considered for a historic district before?

709
01:01:16,906 --> 01:01:19,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to my knowledge, I don't know, Christine.

710
01:01:19,268 --> 01:01:20,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to my knowledge either.

711
01:01:25,932 --> 01:01:26,452
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, so.

712
01:01:26,532 --> 01:01:28,933
[SPEAKER_09]: I think, excuse me, this is Angela.

713
01:01:29,314 --> 01:01:36,478
[SPEAKER_09]: I think it's about time that they do investigate this house because I think this is one of the more important houses to protect.

714
01:01:36,951 --> 01:01:41,274
[SPEAKER_09]: because of its provenance with the two presidents and Abigail Adams.

715
01:01:41,915 --> 01:01:45,137
[SPEAKER_09]: It's just too fragile to lose.

716
01:01:45,978 --> 01:02:01,069
[SPEAKER_09]: I think it's our urgency that we protect this house because, as I said, they've already taken down two houses on Corning Street that were, in my knowledge, older houses, over 200 years old, and just let them be demolished, and it's sad.

717
01:02:01,850 --> 01:02:06,013
[SPEAKER_09]: And they were all in the Smith Farm, and they were all part of that, you know,

718
01:02:07,036 --> 01:02:11,740
[SPEAKER_09]: time period, 1800s, 1700s, as my house is that I live on 16.

719
01:02:12,540 --> 01:02:17,044
[SPEAKER_09]: And I know my house is either late 1700s or early 1800s.

720
01:02:17,384 --> 01:02:23,569
[SPEAKER_09]: I would love my house to be find out exactly how old it is, and be put on a historical commission to save it.

721
01:02:25,550 --> 01:02:29,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, maybe that but and I know the Historical Commission has been very diligent.

722
01:02:29,533 --> 01:02:31,155
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know when these houses were taken down.

723
